Architecture plan for issue #33: Diagnose the deployment mismatch

Product source
- Product/brand name: myClawTeam, from PRODUCT.md.
- The repository is an Astro static landing page for myClawTeam.

Issue scope
- Diagnose why the public deployment URL shows the "Sprite Deployment" placeholder instead of the built Astro site.
- Check which branch and commit are deployed.
- Check whether the build ran successfully.
- Check which directory the deployment serves, specifically whether it serves Astro's dist/ output.

Project structure
- src/pages/index.astro is the Astro entry page and composes the public landing page.
- src/layouts/BaseLayout.astro provides the shared HTML shell and metadata.
- src/components/ contains the page sections: Nav, Hero, FeatureGrid, Community, ContactForm, and Footer.
- src/data/ and src/config/ hold static section data and form configuration.
- src/styles/global.css and tailwind.config.mjs define the visual system.
- scripts/smoke-test.mjs verifies the generated static output after npm run build.

Implementation approach for the issue
- First reproduce locally with npm install if needed, npm run build, and npm run smoke.
- Inspect dist/ after build to confirm the Astro output contains the myClawTeam site rather than placeholder content.
- Inspect deployment configuration files and package scripts for build command, output directory, and adapter/static hosting assumptions.
- Compare the deployed URL behavior against the current main commit and local dist/ output.
- Use GitHub commit/status data as needed to identify whether the host deployed the expected branch and commit.
- Document the root cause and minimal fix path in the issue implementation session.

Dependencies between issues
- There is only one current implementation issue, #33, so there are no cross-issue dependencies.
- The diagnosis depends on the existing Astro build scripts and smoke test, not on new application features.

Key design decisions
- Do not change product branding while diagnosing; keep myClawTeam as defined in PRODUCT.md.
- Prefer configuration and deployment fixes over page rewrites unless the diagnosis proves source content is wrong.
- Keep any code changes small and targeted to build/deployment correctness.
- Avoid introducing persistent storage or backend services; this is a static Astro deployment issue.
